  tool='write' path='/01_capture/influential/2026-03-23__hn-agent-kernel.md' content='# Agent Kernel — Minimal Stateful Agent via Markdown + Git\n\n**Source:** Hacker News #47486287\n**Repo:** https://github.com/oguzbilgic/agent-kernel\n**Captured:** 2026-03-23\n\n## Summary\n\nAgent Kernel presents a minimal approach to agent statefulness using only markdown files and git—no framework, no database. The memory structure consists of AGENTS.md as the kernel, IDENTITY.md for agent identity, KNOWLEDGE.md as a knowledge index, plus knowledge/ folder for current state and notes/ for append-only session logs.\n\n## Key Ideas\n\n- **Zero-database approach:** Three markdown files + git repo as the entire memory system\n- **Memory architecture:** Split between kernel (AGENTS.md), identity (IDENTITY.md), knowledge index (KNOWLEDGE.md), state folder (knowledge/), and session logs (notes/)\n- **Persistence mechanism:** Git as the versioning and durability layer\n- **Simplicity:** No framework overhead, no ORM, no external dependencies\n\n## Community Reactions (HN Discussion)\n\n**Positive:**\n- Simplicity and "text files + git" approach resonates\n- No framework overhead appeals to minimalists\n- Recoverability through git history\n\n**Critical:**\n- Append-only notes can pollute future runs with obsolete context\n- Instruction files are weaker than hooks when behavior must be enforced rather than suggested\n- Scalability concerns as session logs grow\n\n## Implications\n\nThe tension between instruction-as-guidance vs. enforced hooks (mentioned in criticism) mirrors challenges in any AGENTS.md-based system—instructions work well for willing agents but may not prevent prompt injection or accidental deviation. Append-only pollution is a real scalability concern for long-running agents. This validates AGENTS.md as a viable kernel pattern but highlights the need for active cleanup and enforcement mechanisms alongside instruction.\n' start_line=0 end_line=0
